msgid "Haskell"
msgstr "Haskell"

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:3
msgctxt "s-723ab9d26686-0001"
msgid "Entries: [⟦CODE_0001⟧](⟦DEST_0002⟧) (one pure function) and [⟦CODE_0003⟧](⟦DEST_0004⟧) (\"Haskell on drugs\": reading the list does the work)."
msgstr "Entradas: [⟦CODE_0001⟧](⟦DEST_0002⟧) (una función pura) y [⟦CODE_0003⟧](⟦DEST_0004⟧) (\"Haskell bajo los efectos de las drogas\": leer la lista hace el trabajo)."

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:5
msgctxt "s-73988f9bae8a-0002"
msgid "For an advanced reading path with traces and experiments, see the [Haskell study guide](⟦DEST_0001⟧)."
msgstr "Para una ruta de lectura avanzada con trazas y experimentos, consulta la [guía de estudio de Haskell](⟦DEST_0001⟧)."

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:7
msgctxt "s-575bea894294-0003"
msgid "What makes Haskell special"
msgstr "Qué hace especial a Haskell"

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:9
msgctxt "s-8cec37215553-0004"
msgid "Haskell is **lazy**: a value is computed only when something demands it. The straight entry uses that for a stop rule that is not a loop condition at all, but *how much of a list you choose to read*."
msgstr "Haskell es **perezoso**: un valor se calcula solo cuando algo lo demanda. La entrada ordinaria usa eso para una regla de parada que no es una condición de bucle, sino *cuánto eliges leer de una lista*."

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:11
msgctxt "s-4edb797a890b-0005"
msgid "⟦SEP_0001⟧ Feature ⟦SEP_0002⟧ Where ⟦SEP_0003⟧ What it teaches ⟦SEP_0004⟧"
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:13
msgctxt "s-f4c53dd5f521-0006"
msgid "⟦SEP_0005⟧ Infinite lists as descriptions ⟦SEP_0006⟧ [⟦CODE_0001⟧ L41](⟦DEST_0002⟧): ⟦CODE_0003⟧ ⟦SEP_0007⟧ ⟦CODE_0004⟧ produces an endless stream. It is a recipe, not data. ⟦SEP_0008⟧"
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:14
msgctxt "s-5ab5ac2219bd-0007"
msgid "⟦SEP_0007⟧ Running sums over a stream ⟦SEP_0008⟧ [L49](⟦DEST_0001⟧): ⟦CODE_0002⟧ ⟦SEP_0009⟧ ⟦CODE_0003⟧ is a running total; ⟦CODE_0004⟧ repeats the categories because the type derives ⟦CODE_0005⟧ and ⟦CODE_0006⟧. ⟦SEP_0010⟧"
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:15
msgctxt "s-afcb746a5ac6-0008"
msgid "⟦SEP_0006⟧ The stop rule is a split ⟦SEP_0007⟧ [L56-59](⟦DEST_0001⟧): ⟦CODE_0002⟧ ⟦SEP_0008⟧ ⟦CODE_0003⟧ reads one card at a time and stops at the first that fails. ⟦CODE_0004⟧ would drop the crossing card; ⟦CODE_0005⟧ hands back both halves. ⟦SEP_0009⟧"
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:16
msgctxt "s-0565632ff70a-0009"
msgid "⟦SEP_0004⟧ Wrapping arithmetic is a feature ⟦SEP_0005⟧ ⟦CODE_0001⟧ uses a 64-bit LCG on ⟦CODE_0002⟧ ⟦SEP_0006⟧ \"mod 2^64\" is free, because ⟦CODE_0003⟧ wraps. ⟦SEP_0007⟧"
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:18
msgctxt "s-f13cf4ffaf3d-0010"
msgid "Lazy IO as control flow"
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:20
msgctxt "s-cc9cfaabc50d-0011"
msgid "Behold the monstrosity"
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:26
msgctxt "s-5a976daf2e56-0012"
msgid "An infinite list of cards. *Forcing the n-th cell prints card n and sends its ⟦CODE_0001⟧ to ⟦CODE_0002⟧.* Nothing else writes a row. No loop, accumulator, counter or explicit recursion."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:28
msgctxt "s-415f5fa29cf9-0013"
msgid "How the hell does that work?"
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:30
msgctxt "s-a6a321d949e7-0014"
msgid "Three tricks stacked."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:32
msgctxt "s-340d6694431c-0015"
msgid ""
"**A list defined in terms of itself.** [L53](⟦DEST_0001⟧): ⟦CODE_0002⟧. ⟦CODE_0003⟧ hands a function its own result, so ⟦CODE_0004⟧. It works because ⟦CODE_0005⟧ needs only one element at a time.\n"
"2. **A knot through the cards.** [L75-78](⟦DEST_0006⟧): ⟦CODE_0007⟧. Card n's total is its hours plus the total *read off card n-1, an element of the list under construction*. ⟦CODE_0008⟧ is ⟦CODE_0009⟧ for IO.\n"
"3. **Effects that wait to be asked for.** [L62-64](⟦DEST_0010⟧): ⟦CODE_0011⟧. Ordinary ⟦CODE_0012⟧ runs every action first, which never finishes on an infinite list. ⟦CODE_0013⟧ means \"run this only when its result is demanded\". After ⟦CODE_0014⟧ returns, nothing has run; each cell is a promise."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:36
msgctxt "s-fbb8a073ab98-0016"
msgid "The stop rule is [⟦CODE_0001⟧ (L82-83)](⟦DEST_0002⟧): ⟦CODE_0003⟧. Reading card n runs ⟦CODE_0004⟧ for card n, so total reads equal total ⟦CODE_0005⟧s by construction."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:38
msgctxt "s-d2bbf037de84-0017"
msgid "What did we just learn?"
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:40
msgctxt "s-3affe40a368a-0018"
msgid "**Evaluation order is part of a program's meaning once effects are involved.** In a pure program it does not matter. Put IO behind a lazy cell and it decides *which rows exist*."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:41
msgctxt "s-b840fadf1c99-0019"
msgid "**Referential transparency is a contract, and ⟦CODE_0001⟧ is where you stop honoring it.**"
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:42
msgctxt "s-166fe45ca0ff-0020"
msgid "**\"When does this run?\" is the same question** as LINQ's deferred execution (see [C#](⟦DEST_0001⟧)). Both are cases where a description is separated from its execution."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:44
msgctxt "s-d3a34cabf25a-0021"
msgid "Tags: ⟦CODE_0001⟧ laziness is **guaranteed** by the language semantics. ⟦CODE_0002⟧ ordering is **implementation-permitted**: the program is correct because card n cannot be forced before card n-1, not because the evaluator promises an order."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:46
msgctxt "s-299b7a6f9cbe-0022"
msgid "Do this in production and you're fired"
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:48
msgctxt "s-632bccd0652f-0023"
msgid "**The README's own war story.** The first draft counted with ⟦CODE_0001⟧, and the program printed ⟦CODE_0002⟧ then crashed with \"handle is closed\". A lazy ⟦CODE_0003⟧ meant nothing forced the cards until the summary was printed, after the pipe was closed. The cards' effects fired *during the output of ⟦CODE_0004⟧*. The fix: [L144](⟦DEST_0005⟧) ⟦CODE_0006⟧."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:49
msgctxt "s-2b121dac4d57-0024"
msgid "**⟦CODE_0001⟧ must defer its own pattern match.** If the ⟦CODE_0002⟧ runs before ⟦CODE_0003⟧, forcing it needs ⟦CODE_0004⟧, which needs ⟦CODE_0005⟧, which does not exist yet: ⟦CODE_0006⟧."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:50
msgctxt "s-4eefe9e01a62-0025"
msgid "**⟦CODE_0001⟧ fires one more effect.** The crossing card has to be read to see that it crosses, so its row is written. Laziness makes the off-by-one visible."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:51
msgctxt "s-64ecf2f196b6-0026"
msgid "**Testing and concurrency.** Effects whose timing depends on the consumer cannot be tested without the consumer, and cannot be shared safely."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:53
msgctxt "s-8b3cd99bb76e-0027"
msgid "Legitimate uses: lazy IO exists for streaming (⟦CODE_0001⟧ over a large input) and the same hazards are why people moved to streaming libraries. Knot-tying (⟦CODE_0002⟧) is a respectable technique for self-referential data, such as circular structures and memo tables."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:55
msgctxt "s-5e3bcdfbf845-0028"
msgid "Now break it yourself"
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:57
msgctxt "s-79e45855763b-0029"
msgid "See [exercises](⟦DEST_0001⟧): replace ⟦CODE_0002⟧ with plain IO, or move the ⟦CODE_0003⟧, and predict the output."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:59
msgctxt "s-12d0b77aba94-0030"
msgid "Quirks"
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:61
msgctxt "s-1471dae9a8b3-0031"
msgid "**⟦CODE_0001⟧ is not enough**: it drops the crossing card. ⟦CODE_0002⟧ keeps both halves."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:62
msgctxt "s-e271a99c512e-0032"
msgid "**Partial functions.** ⟦CODE_0001⟧, ⟦CODE_0002⟧ and ⟦CODE_0003⟧ crash on an empty list; neither program uses them."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:63
msgctxt "s-4186515f58f9-0033"
msgid "**⟦CODE_0001⟧ vs ⟦CODE_0002⟧ vs ⟦CODE_0003⟧.** The generator wants exactly 64 unsigned bits, so ⟦CODE_0004⟧, whose wraparound is the feature."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:64
msgctxt "s-da61e83667dc-0034"
msgid "**No random package ships with GHC.** Both entries hand-roll a linear congruential generator (Knuth's MMIX constants), using the high bits."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:65
msgctxt "s-59eaa6a7ac25-0035"
msgid "**⟦CODE_0001⟧-style surprises.** The weird entry turns Haskell's most notorious lazy-IO surprise into the algorithm."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:66
msgctxt "s-accb92ce13f8-0036"
msgid "**Style tools.** The source is laid out by hand in Ormolu style and compiles clean under ⟦CODE_0001⟧, but was *not* run through ⟦CODE_0002⟧ or ⟦CODE_0003⟧."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:67
msgctxt "s-c5fb6b0468be-0037"
msgid "**⟦CODE_0001⟧ as a constraint.** [⟦CODE_0002⟧](⟦DEST_0003⟧) bans ⟦CODE_0004⟧, requires ⟦CODE_0005⟧ and exactly one ⟦CODE_0006⟧."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:69
msgctxt "s-a6b58da9d4cd-0038"
msgid "Weird next to boring"
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:71
msgctxt "s-339d0c45ec05-0039"
msgid "[⟦CODE_0001⟧](⟦DEST_0002⟧) is a pure function plus plumbing: easy to test, easy to reason about. [⟦CODE_0003⟧](⟦DEST_0004⟧) puts the effects inside the data structure. The weird version reveals something the idiomatic one hides: a lazy list is a *program waiting to be read*."
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:73
msgctxt "s-c08a852d0dbe-0040"
msgid "Under what circumstances would this terrible idea become reasonable?"
msgstr ""

#: lessons/languages/haskell.md:75
msgctxt "s-99de46342a4a-0041"
msgid "Inside a library that implements lazy streaming and has been carefully tested for ordering. Not in application code, where ⟦CODE_0001⟧, strict IO and streaming libraries give the same results without the surprises."
msgstr ""

